Overview
This practical implements a neural network using TensorFlow/Keras to demonstrate forward propagation and backpropagation for handwritten digit classification. 
It also analyzes how different learning rates and numbers of epochs affect model performance.

Dataset – MNIST
MNIST is a widely used dataset for handwritten digit classification.
Total images: 70,000
Training images: 60,000
Testing images: 10,000
Image size: 28 × 28 pixels
Image type: Grayscale
Number of classes: 10
Classes: Digits 0–9
Pixel range: 0–255
Each image contains a handwritten digit from 0 to 9. The images are reshaped from 28 × 28 into a vector of 784 values and normalized to the range 0–1.

Neural Network
The model contains:
Input layer: 784 input features
Hidden layer: 128 neurons with ReLU
Hidden layer: 64 neurons with ReLU
Output layer: 10 neurons with Softmax
The model uses the Adam optimizer and Sparse Categorical Crossentropy loss.

Learning Rate Analysis
Three learning rates are tested:
0.1
0.01
0.001
The models are trained for 5 epochs and their test accuracies are compared.

Epoch Analysis
Three training durations are tested:
5 epochs
10 epochs
20 epochs
The test accuracy is compared to observe how increasing the number of training epochs affects performance.
